WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:03.690 line:15%
I read an article recently in InStyle magazine,

2
00:00:03.690 --> 00:00:06.130
that made a light bulb go off.

3
00:00:06.130 --> 00:00:09.620
Yes. I had an aha moment.

4
00:00:09.620 --> 00:00:11.170
They put out an article

5
00:00:11.170 --> 00:00:14.720
about the idea of a divorce registry,

6
00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:16.370
where friends and family can come together

7
00:00:16.370 --> 00:00:18.370
to help an individual replace items

8
00:00:18.370 --> 00:00:20.040
around the house that they lost

9
00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:21.060
in a divorce.

10
00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:23.130
It's to celebrate a fresh start

11
00:00:23.130 --> 00:00:25.800
the way we celebrate new beginnings, like a wedding.

12
00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:29.210
And I'm just so excited to talk about this today

13
00:00:29.210 --> 00:00:31.640
with an incredible mom of two.

14
00:00:31.640 --> 00:00:33.882
Please welcome Lori Simonelli.

15
00:00:33.882 --> 00:00:37.370
(audience applauding)
Hi.

16
00:00:37.370 --> 00:00:38.232
Hi Lori.

17
00:00:38.232 --> 00:00:39.650
Hi.
How are you?

18
00:00:39.650 --> 00:00:43.230
Well, I'm really good because a light bulb went off

19
00:00:43.230 --> 00:00:47.380
and then our amazing producers found you

20
00:00:47.380 --> 00:00:50.280
and you got divorced this year.

21
00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:54.520
And I just want to ask you, how is it going?

22
00:00:54.520 --> 00:00:55.650
How was it for you?

23
00:00:55.650 --> 00:01:00.060
How is your new normal in the times of so many new normals?

24
00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:02.350
Look, it wasn't easy.

25
00:01:02.350 --> 00:01:04.600 line:15%
I'm great right now, but you know,

26
00:01:04.600 --> 00:01:07.250 line:15%
we were married almost 17 years.

27
00:01:07.250 --> 00:01:08.330 line:15%
We were together 20.

28
00:01:08.330 --> 00:01:10.860
So when something like this happens,

29
00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:12.923
you kind of lose your identity.

30
00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:16.292
But my ex's name is Nick

31
00:01:16.292 --> 00:01:18.770
and we pulled out all the stops.

32
00:01:18.770 --> 00:01:22.050
You know, we tried everything to not have a divorce.

33
00:01:22.050 --> 00:01:22.950
We didn't want to do that.

34
00:01:22.950 --> 00:01:24.880
We both believed in marriage.

35
00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:26.630
We tried everything together.

36
00:01:26.630 --> 00:01:28.420
As a couple, we realized

37
00:01:28.420 --> 00:01:30.810
we can't put those pieces back together.

38
00:01:30.810 --> 00:01:33.960
And so we, you know, we finally came to the realization

39
00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:36.880
that we needed to kind of end this chapter.

40
00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:40.190
Well, it's funny that you use that word chapter,

41
00:01:40.190 --> 00:01:43.230
because life is this unwritten book.

42
00:01:43.230 --> 00:01:44.920
And I've had to convince myself

43
00:01:44.920 --> 00:01:46.850
of these things when I was like 13

44
00:01:46.850 --> 00:01:49.190
and washed up and just a waste toy.

45
00:01:49.190 --> 00:01:50.470
And I was like, I felt

46
00:01:50.470 --> 00:01:53.430
like that chapter would be my narrative forever.

47
00:01:53.430 --> 00:01:55.890
But you realize that the book keeps getting written

48
00:01:55.890 --> 00:01:57.380
and you're actually the writer.

49
00:01:57.380 --> 00:02:00.100
And this is a chapter.
Right.

50
00:02:00.100 --> 00:02:03.663
Maybe the closing of one, the opening of the next.

51
00:02:04.610 --> 00:02:07.250
I appreciate that you use that word.

52
00:02:07.250 --> 00:02:10.153
I think it symbolizes a lot of things.

53
00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:13.470
What has been a difficult aspect

54
00:02:13.470 --> 00:02:17.340
about this that you didn't foresee?

55
00:02:17.340 --> 00:02:19.560
You know, I think probably one of the hardest things

56
00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:21.850
I saw was the first email that came through,

57
00:02:21.850 --> 00:02:23.770
that said, "Simonelli versus Simonelli".

58
00:02:23.770 --> 00:02:24.950
And I said, "What is this?"

59
00:02:24.950 --> 00:02:26.330
Like we're Nick and Lori,

60
00:02:26.330 --> 00:02:27.950
we're not Simonelli versus Simonelli.

61
00:02:27.950 --> 00:02:30.830
So that was very difficult to process.

62
00:02:30.830 --> 00:02:33.340
And then you had the moments where you are speaking

63
00:02:33.340 --> 00:02:36.650
through attorneys, and it's just very impersonal.

64
00:02:36.650 --> 00:02:38.510
But Nick and I always tried to kind of

65
00:02:38.510 --> 00:02:39.950
like bring it back down.

66
00:02:39.950 --> 00:02:42.770
And we talked throughout everything.

67
00:02:42.770 --> 00:02:44.950
We lived in the same house throughout the whole divorce.

68
00:02:44.950 --> 00:02:46.763
We ate dinner with our kids.

69
00:02:47.940 --> 00:02:51.200
So, you know, that was hard with attorneys.

70
00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:52.650
And one of the hardest things was sitting

71
00:02:52.650 --> 00:02:53.850
down with the kids.

72
00:02:53.850 --> 00:02:57.960
Yeah. And this article said, when someone gets divorced,

73
00:02:57.960 --> 00:03:01.440
often their friends and social circles wanna show up.

74
00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:04.430
But they are not quite sure how.

75
00:03:04.430 --> 00:03:08.620
And a registry gave something concrete to point to.

76
00:03:08.620 --> 00:03:11.080
And that was where the light bulb went off for me.

77
00:03:11.080 --> 00:03:13.090
I thought, why is there not a registry?

78
00:03:13.090 --> 00:03:15.830
Because inevitably in, I would say the majority

79
00:03:15.830 --> 00:03:17.940
of divorces, things are divvied up.

80
00:03:17.940 --> 00:03:20.703
Sometimes people have catastrophic losses.

81
00:03:21.670 --> 00:03:24.003
But what did your friends do for you?

82
00:03:25.410 --> 00:03:29.600 line:15%
You know, I have a few very very close friends

83
00:03:29.600 --> 00:03:31.320
and they were just there.

84
00:03:31.320 --> 00:03:33.080
They were there to listen.

85
00:03:33.080 --> 00:03:34.823
They just made me laugh.
Yeah.

86
00:03:35.660 --> 00:03:38.120
You know, look, they knew that we had,

87
00:03:38.120 --> 00:03:40.010
that Nick and I had a great relationship.

88
00:03:40.010 --> 00:03:43.220
So there was never any bad feelings.

89
00:03:43.220 --> 00:03:45.070
I mean, they're still friendly with Nick.

90
00:03:45.070 --> 00:03:46.400
Right. Well, I think that you

91
00:03:46.400 --> 00:03:48.740
and Nick have a very rare amicability

92
00:03:48.740 --> 00:03:49.920
in this circumstance.
We do.

93
00:03:49.920 --> 00:03:50.753
We're lucky.

94
00:03:50.753 --> 00:03:53.600
Please do a Ted talk, Lori.
(Lori and the audience laugh)

95
00:03:53.600 --> 00:03:55.200
I think it'll get a lot of hits.

96
00:03:56.540 --> 00:04:00.770
And your friends, Julie, Melissa and Hillary,

97
00:04:00.770 --> 00:04:02.870
they've been watching this whole time and they're here.

98
00:04:02.870 --> 00:04:04.419
Hi ladies.

99
00:04:04.419 --> 00:04:05.963
[Julie, Melissa and Hillary] Hi.

100
00:04:05.963 --> 00:04:08.010
Oh my God!

101
00:04:08.010 --> 00:04:09.033
There they are!

102
00:04:09.033 --> 00:04:11.840
(audience applauding)

103
00:04:11.840 --> 00:04:14.440
And we talked all about you

104
00:04:14.440 --> 00:04:16.670
and we have a little surprise for you.

105
00:04:16.670 --> 00:04:17.803
Please watch this.

106
00:04:18.983 --> 00:04:20.531
Hi.

107
00:04:20.531 --> 00:04:22.180
Thanks you guys so much.

108
00:04:22.180 --> 00:04:24.080
It's really, really nice to meet you.

109
00:04:24.080 --> 00:04:25.610 line:15%
Thank you for doing this for Lori.

110
00:04:25.610 --> 00:04:27.580 line:15%
She is the most amazing person

111
00:04:27.580 --> 00:04:30.130
and she would give the shirt off her back

112
00:04:30.130 --> 00:04:31.673
to any of her friends.

113
00:04:31.673 --> 00:04:34.310
I think there's only one silver lining

114
00:04:34.310 --> 00:04:36.530
of any breakup and it's girlfriends.

115
00:04:36.530 --> 00:04:38.540 line:15%
I'm there for her whenever she needs me

116
00:04:38.540 --> 00:04:39.860 line:15%
to talk or laugh.

117
00:04:39.860 --> 00:04:40.910
I just love her to death.

118
00:04:40.910 --> 00:04:42.280
She's such a good person.

119
00:04:42.280 --> 00:04:43.850 line:15%
So every day on my way to work,

120
00:04:43.850 --> 00:04:45.630 line:15%
I would call her for like a half an hour chat

121
00:04:45.630 --> 00:04:46.463
on the way in.

122
00:04:46.463 --> 00:04:48.850
And you know, most days she would start off crying.

123
00:04:48.850 --> 00:04:51.020
And at the end we were both laughing just because, you know

124
00:04:51.020 --> 00:04:52.960
to remind her like her, life's not over.

125
00:04:52.960 --> 00:04:55.370
It's, you know, a new beginning and think of it that way.

126
00:04:55.370 --> 00:04:57.890
I know when Lori had gotten divorced

127
00:04:57.890 --> 00:04:59.320
she was shocked.

128
00:04:59.320 --> 00:05:00.787
When you're getting divorced, you're like,

129
00:05:00.787 --> 00:05:02.820
"I'm crying, everything's cool."

130
00:05:02.820 --> 00:05:03.770
But maybe it's not.

131
00:05:04.690 --> 00:05:05.523
Yeah.

132
00:05:05.523 --> 00:05:09.170
Why are we allowed to have a registry for weddings?

133
00:05:09.170 --> 00:05:11.623
I thought a divorce registry.

134
00:05:13.860 --> 00:05:14.970
It's amazing.

135
00:05:14.970 --> 00:05:16.740
Why hasn't anybody done this so far?

136
00:05:16.740 --> 00:05:18.240
I know. When I went through a divorce,

137
00:05:18.240 --> 00:05:19.637
we just kind of went through the house, like,

138
00:05:19.637 --> 00:05:22.710
"Okay, you take this, I'll take this. You take this."

139
00:05:22.710 --> 00:05:25.340
So I know she's missing a lot of stuff.

140
00:05:25.340 --> 00:05:27.100
And especially in her kitchen.

141
00:05:27.100 --> 00:05:28.860
When I think of registries

142
00:05:28.860 --> 00:05:31.370
I immediately go to kitchen staff.

143
00:05:31.370 --> 00:05:33.160
Is there anything else she's missing

144
00:05:33.160 --> 00:05:37.410
or lacking like bedding or pillows or soft items?

145
00:05:37.410 --> 00:05:40.914
Lori is definitely like farmhouse kind of style.

146
00:05:40.914 --> 00:05:43.510
(indistinct) Lori's, you know, kind of taste.

147
00:05:43.510 --> 00:05:46.383
She loves peanut M&amp;M's in like the prettiest bowl.

148
00:05:46.383 --> 00:05:47.556
In a bowl?
Yeah.

149
00:05:47.556 --> 00:05:51.500
I know like something that she absolutely has been saying

150
00:05:51.500 --> 00:05:54.460
like a million times that she needs is, you know, a console.

151
00:05:54.460 --> 00:05:58.120
Yeah. I have to say, I like both of these

152
00:05:58.120 --> 00:05:59.903
and they're incredibly affordable.

153
00:06:01.470 --> 00:06:05.650
I believe Lori wanted to be able to kind of DIY herself.

154
00:06:05.650 --> 00:06:08.400
I thought this vacuum was super smart.

155
00:06:08.400 --> 00:06:11.700
That will honestly be probably Lori's favorite thing.

156
00:06:11.700 --> 00:06:14.980
Perfect. I think, we get through it with our friends.

157
00:06:14.980 --> 00:06:17.150
So thank you guys so much

158
00:06:17.150 --> 00:06:21.080
for doing this with me and letting me come into your circle.

159
00:06:21.080 --> 00:06:22.443
Let's do this.

160
00:06:24.174 --> 00:06:25.620
(audience applauding)

161
00:06:25.620 --> 00:06:28.200
I just loved meeting your friends.

162
00:06:28.200 --> 00:06:29.487
Hi ladies.

163
00:06:29.487 --> 00:06:31.690
[Julie, Melissa and Hillary] Hi.

164
00:06:31.690 --> 00:06:32.770
What do you think, Lori?

165
00:06:32.770 --> 00:06:35.120
What are you thinking, feeling?

166
00:06:35.120 --> 00:06:36.293
They're amazing.

167
00:06:37.670 --> 00:06:41.590
I mean, Hillary, I've known forever.

168
00:06:41.590 --> 00:06:43.860
I've known her since high school.

169
00:06:43.860 --> 00:06:46.083
And she's amazing.

170
00:06:47.260 --> 00:06:52.260
Julie, she's like the most unbelievable person.

171
00:06:53.350 --> 00:06:56.780
And Melissa, Melissa is me times 10.

172
00:06:56.780 --> 00:06:58.210
We always laugh about it.

173
00:06:58.210 --> 00:06:59.870
My kids call her crazy Melissa.

174
00:06:59.870 --> 00:07:01.680
She comes in my house and we laugh from

175
00:07:01.680 --> 00:07:04.490
the moment she comes in till the moment she leaves

176
00:07:04.490 --> 00:07:06.510
and my kids never come out of the rooms.

177
00:07:06.510 --> 00:07:08.920
But when they hear her, they're like, "Melissa's here,"

178
00:07:08.920 --> 00:07:10.220
and they run out.

179
00:07:10.220 --> 00:07:13.010
This was so great to do this with you guys.

180
00:07:13.010 --> 00:07:16.100
And we created this registry

181
00:07:16.100 --> 00:07:19.450
and we purchased every single item on it.

182
00:07:19.450 --> 00:07:20.699
So let's--

183
00:07:20.699 --> 00:07:25.200
(audience applauding)
(lighthearted guitar music)

184
00:07:25.200 --> 00:07:27.680
This is all for you, Lori.

185
00:07:27.680 --> 00:07:31.150
And Melissa, Julie, Hillary and I,

186
00:07:31.150 --> 00:07:33.180
just thought thoughtfully

187
00:07:33.180 --> 00:07:37.010
about a few little goodies that'll get you going

188
00:07:37.010 --> 00:07:38.710
and--
A few?

189
00:07:38.710 --> 00:07:39.970
Yeah, exactly.

190
00:07:39.970 --> 00:07:41.050
Oh my God!

191
00:07:41.050 --> 00:07:42.030
Well, I have to tell you,

192
00:07:42.030 --> 00:07:43.900
I don't think I would have had this idea.

193
00:07:43.900 --> 00:07:46.010
If I wasn't working on this company

194
00:07:46.010 --> 00:07:50.120 line:15%
I just created with my partner, Shae Hong, called Beautiful,

195
00:07:50.120 --> 00:07:52.413 line:15%
which is all kitchen stuff.

196
00:07:53.520 --> 00:07:55.290 line:15%
I'm flabbergasted

197
00:07:55.290 --> 00:07:58.180
and I can't believe these three are here.

198
00:07:58.180 --> 00:08:00.200
It's like overwhelming.

199
00:08:00.200 --> 00:08:03.130 line:15%
Well, there is no clock that measures

200
00:08:03.130 --> 00:08:05.510 line:15%
when things evolve.

201
00:08:05.510 --> 00:08:08.220 line:15%
It's a mysterious clock that just takes time,

202
00:08:08.220 --> 00:08:10.120 line:15%
but our girlfriends get us through it.

203
00:08:10.120 --> 00:08:13.746
So thank you, Julie, Melissa and Hillary and Lori.

204
00:08:13.746 --> 00:08:16.579
(energetic music)

